# TEMPLATE ONLY — this file is committed to git. Never put real values in it.
#
# Copy it to `.env` and fill that in instead:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Vite loads `.env`, not this file, and only reads it at startup — restart the
# dev server after editing. `.env` is gitignored; this one deliberately is not.
#
# Both values come from the Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API Keys
# (or → API on the older layout). Use the key labelled `anon` / `public`, or
# "Publishable key". Those are safe to ship in a browser bundle, which is the
# entire point of the row level security policies in supabase/migrations.
#
# Two things that must NEVER go in here or in `.env`:
#
#   - the `service_role` / Secret key — it bypasses row level security
#   - your Postgres database password — it grants direct database access
#
# Everything in `.env` with a VITE_ prefix is compiled into the JavaScript that
# every visitor downloads. Anything else in there is still sitting in plain text
# on disk in a file that gets copied around. Database credentials belong in a
# password manager, and nowhere in this project.
#
# The site runs fine with these left empty: the portfolio is unaffected and the
# blog renders a "not connected yet" state instead of crashing.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
